full_judgment:
1.56 acre of land of SA Plot Nos. 31, 32 and 31/193 of mouza Baje Kakrail within Police Station Ramna, Dhaka forming Municipal Holding No. 85 Kakrail, Ramna, Dhaka belonged to one Lawrence Aloysius Noronha died intestate on 15-2-63 and his right, title and interest in the said property devolved on his two sons-Clifford Noronha and George Eric Noronha and two daughters-Winifred Rubie and Josephine Willes, who died intestate, as a result half of her share devolved on her husband DR Willes and the rest devolved on her two brothers and one sister. Thereafter Winifred Rubie filed a partition suit being Partition Suit No. 113 of 1996 against DR Willes and others which was dismissed. Winifred Rubie preferred an appeal being FA No. 318 of 1970 before the High Court of the then East Pakistan and the appeal was allowed and the suit was decreed by the High Court of Bangladesh by judgment dated 25-5-97. Winifred Rubie and her two brothers each got 7/24th share and DR Willes got 3/24th share. Thereafter the final decree was passed on 2-5-93 and DR Willes got half of the main building in the western side and the land underneath in his 3/24th share. Rest of the property was allotted to the saham of the surviving sons and daughters of Laurence Aloysius Noronha. The aforesaid property was requisitioned under the provisions of Emergency Requisition of Property Act. 1948 by an order dated 19-7-77 for accommodation of Willes Little Flower School. The said requisition was challenged by Winifred Rubie, but was found by the Appellate Division to have been validly made. The petitioners and late Moshahid Ali Chowdhury 16 (sic) executed and registered between 20-7-81 and 9-9-81 purchased the entire share of the aforesaid property allotted 21/24th share to Clifford Noronha, George Eric Noronha and Winifred Rubie. Moshahid Ali Chowdhury died in April 1993 leaving behind wife petitioner No. 3, two sons, petitioner Nos. 1 and 2, three daughters, Mrs. Suraiya Islam, Mrs Aleya Chowdhury and Mrs Rabeya Chowdhury as his heirs. The petitioners and late Moshahid Ali Chowdhury, after the aforesaid purchase, applied to the Government of Bangladesh for derequisition of the same. The Government of Bangladesh through the Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms after hearing the petitioners and the Willes Little Flower School authority on 16-1-85 issued an order of derequisition directing the Deputy Commissioner, respondent No. 2, to release specified and demarcated 0.63 acre of the aforesaid property together with the building standing thereon and deliver possession of the derequisitioned property to the &quot;original owners from whom the property was requisitioned or to their successor-in-interest after verification of title. A true copy of the said order dated 16-10-85 is annexed to the petition and marked as Annexure &quot;A&quot;. Thereafter, the Additional Deputy Commissioner (LA) respondent No. 1 issued a notice under Rule 10(1) of the Emergency Requisition of Property Rules for verification of title and after verification of the title documents and other papers produced by the petitioners respondent No. 1 was satisfied about the title of the petitioners and late Moshahid Ali Chowdhury to the aforesaid property. The names of the petitioners and late Moshahid Ali Chowdhury were mutated in the Revenue recorls of the Government of Bangladesh in respect of the aforesaid property and the rent of the said property was accepted by the Government. The true copies of the mutation order, Mutation DCR, Mutation Khatian and the rent receipt are annexed to the petition and marked as Annexures B, B(1), B(2), B(3) and B(4). Thereafter the Additional Deputy Commissioner (LA) issued notice upon the petitioners and late Moshahid Ali Chowdhury under rule 10(2) of the Emergency Requisition of Property Rules in Form E on 26-12-85. True copy of the said notice is annexed to the petition and marked as Annexure-G. Respondent No. 1 also issued a Memo dated 26-12-85 (true copy of which is annexed to the petition and marked as Annexure-D by which he requested the Head Mistress of the said School to deliver vacant possession of the derequisitioned property to Surveyor of Land Acquisition Office. The possession could not be taken over due to the obstruction created by the School authority. On 13-1-86 the Additional Deputy Commissioner wrote to the Head Mistress of the school requesting her to hand over vacant possession on 16-1-86. True copy of the said Memo is annexed to the petition and marked as Annexure &quot;E&quot;. On 16-1-86 the officials of Dhaka Collect orate went to take over the possession of the derequisitioned property, but possession could not be taken due to obstruction created by the school authority. The Head Mistress of the said school filed a suit being title suit No. 3 of 1986 in the Third Court of Subordinate Judge, Dhaka against the Government of Bangladesh and respondent No. 1 praying for a declaration that the order dated 26-12-85 (Annexure-&quot;C&quot;) issued by respondent No. 1 was illegal and of no legal effect. A true copy of the plaint of the said suit is annexed to the petition and marked as Annexure-&quot;F&#39;&#39;. In the said suit the Government filed written statement stating, inter (ilia, that Moshahid Ali Chowdhury and 5 others purchased the suit property by 60 registered sale deeds with an area of 1.56 acre of land covering the holding No. 85 Kakrail Road out of which the share of Clifford Noronha measuring an area 0.56 acre of land was declared as vested in VP Case No. 5 of 1974. The Ministry of LA and LR took decision on 16-10-85 on the application submitted by Mr. Moshahid Ali Chowdhury, in presence&quot; of the Head Mistress and the Members of the School Managing Committee, the Additional Deputy Commissioner, Dhaka Division communicated the same under Memo No. DA-54/84/835 dated 16-10-85 for implementation. As per decision of the Ministry of LA and LR dated 16-10-85 the school was asked to hand over possession of the derequisitioned portion to the representative of the requisitioning authority on 29-12-85 but the possession could not be taken as the Head Mistress showed an injunction passed in TS 1244/85 of the 4th Court of Munsif, Dhaka. Subsequently in Misc. Appeal No. 352/85 in the court of the District Judge Dhaka an order staying operation of impugned injunction in TS 1244/85 was passed. The plaintiff was informed regarding derequisition of 0.63 1/2 acre of land out of 1.50 1/2 as per order passed by the Ministry of LA and LR under Memo No. 2226/Reqn/85 dated. 26-12-85. Having failed to obtain possession of the derequisitioned portion of the aforesaid property the petitioners and late Moshahid Ali Chowdhury filed Writ Petition No. 512 of 1987 for implementation of the derequisition order passed by the Government on 16-10-85. After hearing this Court by judgment dated 13-12-90 made the Rule absolute passing the following order&nbsp; 

 A true copy of the judgment dated 13-12-90 is annexed to the petition and marked as Annexure-&quot;H&quot;. The Government filed leave application before the Appellate Division against the judgment of the High Court Division and similar application was also filed by the School Authority. The Appellate Division after hearing was unwilling to grant leave and, as such, the Government and the School Authority withdrew their respective leave applications and filed review applications before the High Court Division. The High Court Division rejected the review application and against the said rejection order the Government and also the School Authority filed leave applications before the Appellate Division which was rejected by order dated 6-3-91. A true copy of the order of the Appellate Division dated 6-3-91 is annexed to the petition and marked as Annexure-I. The respondents did not comply with the order of the Court and instead the Land Acquisition Officer on 8-4-91 served notice dated 1-4-91 under Rule (10)1 of the Emergency Requisition of Property Rules stating therein that enquiry would be held on 10-4-91 in respect of right and title of the aforesaid derequisitioned property and requiring the petitioners to submit statement regarding their title on 9-4-91 and to be present with papers on 10-4-91. The petitioners submitted a petition before respondent No. 2 saying that formalities and requirements of rule 10(1) of the aforesaid Rules had been completed long before and the petitioners were given notice under Rule 10(2) of the said Rules and the Officers of the District Administration twice endeavored to hand over possession of the derequisitioned property but failed due to the obstructions created by the School Authority and therefore question of determining right, title and interest in the aforesaid property did not arise. It was alleged that the notice dated 1-4-91 was nothing but a device and cloak to delay and/or avoid implementation of the Judgment of the High Court Division passed in Writ Petition No. 512 of 1987. On 10-4-91 the petitioner No. 1 appeared before the Land Acquisition Officer. In this situation the petitioners and late Moshahid Ali Chowdhury were compelled to file a wit petition for drawing up contempt proceeding against the Deputy Commissioner, Dhaka, Additional Deputy Commissioner (LA), Dhaka, Land Acquisition Officer, Dhaka and Head Master of Willes Little Flower School and the High Court in Contempt Petition No. 15 of 1991 was pleased to draw up the proceeding till final hearing.&nbsp; 

 3. On 7-7-93 the petitioners saw a news item in Daily Star about a function of the managing committee of the school where the managing committee thanked the Government for permanently leasing out 0.87 acre of land to the school. Petitioner No. 1 went to the office of respondent No. 1 seeking information. The petitioners and late Moshahid Ali Chowdhury had earlier filed Title Suit No. 246 of 1984, subsequently re-numbered as Title Suit No. 143 of 1988 in the Court of Subordinate Judge, 1st Court, Dhaka against the Deputy Commissioner, Dhaka and others including the school as defendant No. 4. On 30-8-93, the school authority filed an application in that suit stating that 0.87 acre of the aforesaid property had been permanently acquired by the Government in LA Case No. 14 of 1990-91 and it has been transferred to the school authority for valuable consideration of Taka 1,34,50,668/84. A true copy of the said petition is annexed to the petition and marked as Annexure-J (i). After making frantic effort petitioner No. 1 succeeded in finding out the Gazette Notification regarding the alleged acquisition in the first part of November, 1993 and obtained copy of the Gazette notification dated 3-9-92 published in the Bangladesh Gazette which is annexed to the petition and marked as Annexure-K. Annexure-K shows that 0.08 acre of plot No. 31/193, 0.07 acre of plot No. 31 and 0.72 acre of plot No. 32, in total 0.87 acre of land had been acquired under provisions of Acquisition and Requisition of Immovable Property Ordinance 1982 in LA Case No. 14 of 1990-91. It is submitted that for acquisition of any immovable property section 3 of the Ordinance requires publication of preliminary notice of acquisition on or near the property in the prescribed form, but no such notice was issued to the knowledge of the petitioners. Had any such notice been published in any convenient place, the petitioners would have known about it. The petitioners have every reason to believe that in collusion with the school authority respondent Nos. 1-2 may have shown publication of such notice on record without actually publishing any such notice at any convenient place of the property in question so as to keep the petitioners in dark about it. It is further submitted that section 6 of the Ordinance requires that after making decision for acquiring property public notice of the proposed acquisition has to be given in the prescribed manner and convenient places on or near the property and section 6(3) of the Ordinance requires service of such notice on all persohs known or believed to be interested therein. The petitioners are certainly interested in the said property and the respondents also knew about it. Not only that no such notice was published at convenient places on or near the property in question, no notice under section 6(3) of the Ordinance was served on the petitioners even though the respondents had full knowledge that the petitioners are owners of the said property. It is alleged that the respondents had fraudulently and collusively with the school authority omitted to publish and serve such notices so that the petitioner might not be able to raise any lawful objection to the acquisition and the property could not be acquired for a price below the price permissible under the Ordinance and to prevent them from referring the matter to arbitration. That no notice of passing of the award made by the respondents was given to the petitioners. Everything had been done secretly and without complying with the requirements of the law. After coming to know of the acquisition from the petition submitted by the school authority in the aforesaid suit, petitioner No. 1 met respondent No. 1 and wanted to see the award, but respondent No. 1 flatly refused to show it to him. It is also submitted that the holding of 85, Kakrail, Dhaka is situated on the Kakrail Road. The property in question is situated in the commercial centre of Dhaka and the land is being sold in this locality at the rate of Taka 12 to 15 lakh per katha and the value of 0.87 acre of the acquired land alone without the structure would not be less than Taka 7 crore. Because the people under valued the sale price of properties at Dhaka the Government issued notification stating the minimum price of lands in different localities in Dhaka city for the purpose of payment of stamp duty, gift tax, etc. The price so stated in the notification is much below the actual market price. On 3-1-91 the Government by Gazette Notification refixed the price of lands at Dhaka and photocopy of the Gazette Notification is annexed to the petition and marked as Annexure-L. Again on 20-12-92 the Government refixed the price and by a circular dated 11-1-93 stated the existing price and re-fixed price. True copy of the said circular dated 11-1-93 is annexed to the petition and marked as Annexure L(1). It is seen from the Gazette Notification dated 3-1-91 and the circular dated 20-12-92 that the Government stated the minimum price of land on the main road side in Kakrail area to be Taka 1 crone per bigha which means Taka 5 lakh per katha. At the Government stated rate the price of 0.87 acre of land alone was at least Taka 2,63,63,636.00 but the respondents assessed the compensation of 0.87 acre together with the building standing thereon at Taka 1,34,50,668.84 as stated by the school authority. The Government having fixed the minimum rate of price of land and publishing the notification dated 4-12-90 in the official gazette if the respondent acted bona fide they could not have assessed compensation at half the rate fixed by the Government. The whole transaction of the respondents smacks of mala fide and the gross under assessment of compensation explains why the respondents kept the petitioners completely in the dark about the transaction which clearly established the collusion of the respondents 1-2 with the school authority. The respondents are so biased that they gave no notice to the petitioners so as to deprive the petitioners an opportunity of seeking arbitration against the gross under assessment. The acquiring authority having not complied with the requirement of law, the entire process of the acquisition of the property in question is illegal and without lawful authority and has not been any lawful acquisition in the eye of law.&nbsp; 

 4. An affidavit-in-opposition has been filed on behalf of the respondent Nos. 1-3. It appears that the respondents have admitted that the property in question belonged to one Laurence Alloysius Noronha and his heirs. The respondents however dispute the alleged transfers of property by the heirs in favour of the petitioners and late Moshahid Ali Chowdhury. It is stated that the alleged transfer were made by non-residents by means of various deeds registered in seven sub-registry Offices in Dhaka simultaneously and consecutively ranging between 20-7-81 and 9-9-81 valued only at Taka fifty thousand each, obviously for avoiding payment of Gain-Tax to the Government. The petitioners have not stated why sixty documents had to be created in respect of 4-1/3 bighas land for taking transfer from the owners. Clifford Eric Noronha and George Eric Noronha did not execute and register any instrument of transfer. The alleged powers of attorney by virtue of which the transfer documents were executed and registered are not genuine and these were not signed or executed or given by Clifford Noronha and/or George Eric Norona. The statement made in paragraph 7 of the petition has been denied. It is stated that after issuance of the order of de-requisition dated 16-10-85 by the Government, enquiry which is the mandatory requirement of the (Emergency) Requisition of Property Rules, 1948 was not made to ascertain as to who are entitled to restoration of possession of the property and notice under Rule 10(1). It is stated that the notice under the Emergency Requisition of Property Rules in prescribed form was issued without verification of title. It is also stated that some Civil Suits relating to the title of the acquisitioned land are pending for adjudication in the civil Court. Since the petitioners were not in possession they were not served with notice under section 6 of the Land Acquisition Ordinance, 1982. (a) Mrs Josephine Willes wife of DR Willes (b) Mrs Clifford A Noronha (c) Dr George Eric A. Noronha, son of Lawrence Alloysius Noronha (d) DR Willes son of HJ Willes (e) Mrs. Winifred Ruby daughter of Mr Lawrence Alloysius Noronha who were the undisputed original owners were served with notice under section 6 of the Land Acquisition Ordinance, 1982 at Kakrail 85. As the title to the land could not be established none has been paid compensation. The compensation of the Government was deposited in Government Accounts Deposit Accounts Head under section 10(2) of the Land Acquisition Ordinance, 1982. Since the petitioners&#39;&#39; title to the property under acquisition is sub judice in the civil Court the question of informing them in any matter did not arise at all. It &#39;&#39;is stated that the land in question was decided to be acquired for the project of Willes Little Flower School under PS Ramna within Mouza Baje Kakrail appertaining to SA Plot No. 31 (part) 32 (part) and 31/193 (part) measuring 0.87 acre pending approval of the Central Land Acquisition Committee vide Land Ministry Memo No. Bhu/Mah/Sha-10/Who/ DA-54/84/150/-Acqn. dated 14-3-91. In the light of the Resolution, Land Ordinance No. 2/82 (which was later converted into Land Acquisition Act, 1987) notice under section 3 was served on 24-3-91. After the service of notice under section 3 no objection notice was received within 15 days from the service of notice. The Deputy Commissioner on 4-4-91 accorded permission under section 4(3) for acquisition and according to the provisions of that Act notice was served fixing 22-4-91 for giving written objection within that time but no objection was received within that time. Before the service of notice under section 3, 12 months average value of land on the basis of information collected from the deeds of the Sub-Registrar&#39;&#39;s Office and the price was sanctioned on that basis. The aforesaid project deposited the entire compensation money of Taka 1,34,50,663.00 towards land, structures, doors and trees in favour of the requiring body who. &#39;&#39;deposited the entire compensation money in LA Case No. 14 of 1990-91. Later on, notice was served under section 7, but nobody claimed compensation money. On 4-5-92 land, structures, doors and trees acquired in LA Case No. 14 of 1990-91 were made over to the Land Requiring Body. Since nobody came forward with established title to the property for compensation, so under sub-section 2 of section 10(2) of the Land Acquisition Ordinance of 1982 the compensation money has been kept as Government deposit. Later on, Willes Little Flower School obtained registered deed from the Government in accordance with the relevant law of acquisition. The acquisition has been undertaken for public purpose and public interest and all proper legal steps have been taken. The land acquired in LA 14 of 1990-91 has been published in Gazette Notification in accordance with section 11 and sub-sections of Land Acquisition Ordinance, 1982. The acquisition is therefore valid and for public purpose.&nbsp; 

 7. Supplementary affidavit on behalf of the respondent No. 4 has also been filed stating, inter alia, that in paragraph 18 of the affidavit in opposition of the respondents 1-3 it was stated that civil litigations are pending in competent civil Courts in which the petitioners are parties. The title of the petitioners being very much in dispute and subject to decision in the suits, there was no way the respondent Nos. 1-3 could lawfully restore possession of the derequisitioned property to the petitioners, but as details of such civil litigations had not been given it is therefore stated that the petitioners filed Title Suit No. 143 of 1988 in the First Court of Subordinate Judge, Dhaka, praying for declaration of their title to 14 annas share in the land which is subject matter of the present acquisition. This respondent and the Government of Bangladesh are contesting the suit by filing separate written statements. True copies of the plaint and the written statements are annexed to the supplementary affidavit on behalf of the respondent No. 4 as marked as Annexure-X, X(1) and X(2). This respondent filed Title Suit No. 362 of 1990 in the First Court of Subordinate Judge, Dhaka, challenging the genuineness of title documents by which the petitioners claimed title to the land under acquisition. The petitioners are contesting the suit by filing written statement. True copies of the plaint and the written statement of the said suit are annexed to the supplementary affidavit and marked as Annexure Y and Y(1).&nbsp; 

 ii) the block marked &#39;&#39;A&#39;&#39; in the sketch map falling within SA plot No. 32 with double storied building partly occupied by its original owners and partly under use of the School should be withdrawn from requisition and possession made over to the original owner/owners from whom it was requisition or to his/their successor-in-interest after verification of their right and title.&nbsp; 

 16. It appears to us from the proceedings record of LA case No. 14/90-91 that the procedure for acquiring 0.87 acre of land as required under the Acquisition and Requisition of Immovable Property Ordinance, 1982 has been followed save and except the requirement of sub-section 3 of section 6 of the Ordinance, 1982 which requires notice to be served on all persons known or believed to be interested in the property. We have already expressed our view for reasons given that notice ought to have been served on the petitioners. The proceedings of the LA Case as contained in Annexure-4 series of the supplementary affidavit filed on behalf of the respondent Nos. 1-3 show that notice under section 6 of the Ordinance, 1982 were served on Mrs Josephine Willes, Mrs Clifford A Noronha, Dr George Eric Noronha, DR Willes, Mrs. Winifred Rubie and Secretary, Willes Little Flower School at 85, Kakrail, Rarnna Dhaka who are undisputed original owners of the land. The respondents, as it appears from the affidavit-in-opposition, did not serve notice on the petitioners on the ground that their title had not been proved. There were several suits filed by the petitioners connected with the property including for a suit for declaration that they are the owners of 1.56 acre of land which are still pending. Further, in Annexure-A to the petition it was said that the block marked-A which was to be withdrawn from requisition possession was to be made over to the original owner/owners-from whom it was requisitioned or to his/ their successor-in-interest after verification of their right and title. The land was requisitioned from the original owners and the petitioners were still to establish their title and interest. It was for these reasons the respondents said that no notice was served on the petitioners under section 6 of the Ordinance, 1982, but the respondents having made a claim of ownership of 1.56 acre of land and having also filed a petition against failure to de-requisition 0.63 acre of land as directed by the High Court, the respondents ought to have known or believed that the petitioners are interested parties. The question therefore arises whether under the circumstances non-service of notice under sub-section 3 of section 6 of the Ordinance, 1982 will render the acquisition of property by Annexure-K illegal.&nbsp; 

 17. The learned Counsel appearing for the petitioners has not cited any decision that in default of such notice the acquisition is illegal. It is our considered view that an irregularity in the procedure will not ipso facto render the acquisition illegal provided the acquisition was for a public purpose and the compensation amount was adequate and fixed in accordance with law. In the instant case the petitioners have not seriously challenged that the acquisition was for a public purpose. On the question of awarding the compensation it is correct that the petitioners did not have any notice of the acquisition and were thus deprived of opportunity to file an application to the Arbitrator within 45 days of the date of service of award for revision of the award under section 28 of the Acquisition and Requisition of Immovable Property Ordinance, 1982. However, it appears from the order sheet in LA Case No. 14/90-91 that while fixing the value of 0.87 acre of acquired land the &#39;&#39;Kanungo&#39;&#39; after obtaining information and consultation with the Sub-Registry Office submitted the valuation of the land in that area. It appears from that report and information that the price of Nal land per acre was 1,20,48,200.00 and by adding 50% over that price, the price of bhiti land per acre came to Taka 1,80,72,300.00. It was on this basis that the price of 0.87 acre of land was fixed at Taka 1,34,50,668.00 which amount was duly paid by the requiring body Willes Little Flower School. This amount, it is stated by the respondents, is now kept in the block account as nobody came forward to claim the compensation and the title to the land has not been established.&#39;&#39; This amount has been kept in the block account under sub-section 2 of section 10 of the Acquisition and Requisition of Immovable Property Ordinance, 1982 which provides that if the persons entitled to compensation do not consent to receive it, or if there be no person competent to receive the compensation or if there be any dispute as to title to receive the compensation or as to the apportionment of it, the Deputy Commissioner shall keep the amount of the compensation in a deposit account in the Public Account of the Republic which shall be deemed payment for the purpose of taking over possession of the property without any prejudice to the claim of the parties to be determined by the Arbitrator. The petitioners therefore may claim the compensation to be paid from this account on proof of their interest and title.&nbsp;

The opposite party Nos. 1-10 as plaintiffs instituted Title Suit No. 17 of 2001 before the court of Assistant Judge, Barguna Sadar, Barguna against the petitioner along with proforma opposite party Nos. 11-72 impleading them as defendants for declaration of title and on 16-9-2003 the said title suit No. 17 of 2001 in the court of Assistant Judge, Barguna Sadar was decreed in terms of a Solenama with Opposite Party No. 46 (the defendant No. 41 in title suit No. 17 of 2001) and ex-parte against the rest.&nbsp; 

 3. That in pursuance the petitioner on 17-2-2004 filed Miscellaneous Case No. 4 of 2004 under Order IX, Rule 13 read with section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure of 1908 before the Court of Assistant Judge, Barguna Sadar against the judgment and decree dated 16-9-2003 in Title Suit No. 17 of 2001 contending inter-alia that the summons in title suit No. 17 of 2001 was not served upon the petitioner being defendant No. 43 in Title Suit No. 17 of 2001. That the opposite parties No. 1-10 being petitioners in Title Suit No. 17 of 2001 with collusion with the process server showed that the notice was served upon the petitioner, that is defendant No. 43 in the title suit and thus obtained an ex-parte judgment and decree dated 16-9-2003 and that if the Title Suit No. 17 of 2001 had not been disposed of in the absence of the petitioner, then the petitioner would have contested the suit and as such prayed that the ex-parte judgment and decree dated 6-9-2003 be set-aside being void and inoperative so that the petitioner would get a chance to contest the suit in Title Suit No. 17 of 2001 by restoring the same to its original file and number.&nbsp; 

 4. That the opposite parties No. 1, 3 and 10 being plaintiffs in Title Suit No. 17 of 2001 contested the case denying the material allegations made against them by filing a written objection contending inter-alia that the summons of Title Suit No. 17 of 2001 was properly served upon the defendants and that the petitioner despite being fully aware of the filing of the said Title Suit abstained from contesting the suit because he was apprehensive of failure in the said suit.&nbsp; 

 5. The Learned Senior Assistant Judge upon examination of witnesses from both sides and after hearing both parties allowed the Miscellaneous Case No. 4 of 2004 by his judgment and order dated 6-11-2005 and thus set-aside the earlier judgment and decree dated 16-9-2003 passed in Title Suit No. 17 of 2001 and consequently directed to restore Title Suit No. 17 of 2001 in its original file and number.&nbsp; 

 13. Now in the case presently before me, it is apparent from the records that the petitioner&#39;s major contention is that in the original suit, he was a defendant, yet summons was not duly served upon him and which had therefore compelled him to file the suit under Order IX, Rule 13 and section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure 1908. I have also perused and examined the laws and also looked into the facts surrounding the case and found that the Miscellaneous Case was filed on 17-2-2004 and the plaintiff petitioner in the Miscellaneous. Case had admittedly come to know about the said ex-parte decree for the first time on 9-1-2004. Therefore as is obvious from the records the petitioners received &quot;knowledge&quot; of the decree for the first time on 9-1-2004. Through my perusal of the law, the prescribed period of filing application to set-aside an ex-parte order is &#39;statutorily within 30 days that is the statutory time limit within date of the decree or where the summons was not duly served, the application has to be filed within 30 days of the date when the applicant has &#39;knowledge&#39; of the decree. In the petitioner&#39;s case it is apparent that the prescribed 30 days to file an application ended on 9-2-2004, and, therefore, since the petitioner filed the application on 17-2-2004, it is out of time for about 9 days in filing the miscellaneous case and therefore his case falls within the mischief of Article 164 of the Limitation Act, 1908.&nbsp;
